            <ArticleTitle>Splenic injury after intraoperative transesophageal echocardiography.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>2141-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 55-year-old woman underwent coronary artery bypass grafting and mitral valve repair using intraoperative transesophageal echocardiography (TEE). Postoperatively she had hemodynamic instability associated with an increase in abdominal size and a drop in hemoglobin. At laparotomy, a splenic hilar laceration was discovered, which was presumed to be associated with intraoperative TEE. She underwent emergent splenectomy. This case demonstrates that although rare, serious complications can occur with TEE.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
